## Solution

We compute the Christoffel symbols of the second kind for the 2‑dimensional metric
$$g_{ij}=\begin{bmatrix}1&0\\0&F(x^1,x^2)\end{bmatrix},\qquad g=\det g_{ij}=F>0.$$

### Step 1. Inverse metric

Since the metric is diagonal,
$$g^{11}=1,\qquad g^{22}=\frac{1}{F},\qquad g^{12}=g^{21}=0.$$

### Step 2. Formula

The Christoffel symbols of the second kind are
$$\Gamma^{k}_{ij}=\tfrac12\,g^{kl}\bigl(\partial_i g_{jl}+\partial_j g_{il}-\partial_l g_{ij}\bigr).$$

Denote $F_1:=\partial F/\partial x^1$ and $F_2:=\partial F/\partial x^2$. The only non‑constant component is $g_{22}=F$, so $\partial_1 g_{22}=F_1$, $\partial_2 g_{22}=F_2$, and all other derivatives of $g_{ij}$ vanish.

### Step 3. Symbols with upper index $k=1$

Only $g^{11}=1$ contributes (since $g^{12}=0$):
$$\Gamma^{1}_{ij}=\tfrac12\bigl(\partial_i g_{j1}+\partial_j g_{i1}-\partial_1 g_{ij}\bigr).$$

- $\Gamma^{1}_{11}=\tfrac12(\partial_1 g_{11}+\partial_1 g_{11}-\partial_1 g_{11})=\tfrac12\,\partial_1 g_{11}=0.$
- $\Gamma^{1}_{12}=\Gamma^{1}_{21}=\tfrac12(\partial_1 g_{21}+\partial_2 g_{11}-\partial_1 g_{12})=0.$
- $\Gamma^{1}_{22}=\tfrac12(\partial_2 g_{21}+\partial_2 g_{21}-\partial_1 g_{22})=\tfrac12(0+0-F_1)=-\dfrac{F_1}{2}.$

### Step 4. Symbols with upper index $k=2$

Only $g^{22}=1/F$ contributes:
$$\Gamma^{2}_{ij}=\frac{1}{2F}\bigl(\partial_i g_{j2}+\partial_j g_{i2}-\partial_2 g_{ij}\bigr).$$

- $\Gamma^{2}_{11}=\dfrac{1}{2F}(\partial_1 g_{12}+\partial_1 g_{12}-\partial_2 g_{11})=0.$
- $\Gamma^{2}_{12}=\Gamma^{2}_{21}=\dfrac{1}{2F}(\partial_1 g_{22}+\partial_2 g_{12}-\partial_2 g_{12})=\dfrac{1}{2F}(F_1+0-0)=\dfrac{F_1}{2F}.$

  (More carefully: with $i=1,j=2,l=2$, the bracket is $\partial_1 g_{22}+\partial_2 g_{12}-\partial_2 g_{12}=F_1+0-0=F_1$.)

- $\Gamma^{2}_{22}=\dfrac{1}{2F}(\partial_2 g_{22}+\partial_2 g_{22}-\partial_2 g_{22})=\dfrac{1}{2F}\,F_2=\dfrac{F_2}{2F}.$

### Step 5. Result

Collecting the non‑vanishing components (all others are zero):

$$\boxed{\Gamma^{1}_{22}=-\frac{1}{2}\frac{\partial F}{\partial x^1},\qquad \Gamma^{2}_{12}=\Gamma^{2}_{21}=\frac{1}{2F}\frac{\partial F}{\partial x^1},\qquad \Gamma^{2}_{22}=\frac{1}{2F}\frac{\partial F}{\partial x^2}.}$$

All remaining symbols vanish:
$$\Gamma^{1}_{11}=\Gamma^{1}_{12}=\Gamma^{1}_{21}=\Gamma^{2}_{11}=0.$$

### Verification (geodesic consistency)

The geodesic equations $\ddot x^k+\Gamma^k_{ij}\dot x^i\dot x^j=0$ read
$$\ddot x^1-\tfrac12 F_1\,(\dot x^2)^2=0,\qquad \ddot x^2+\frac{F_1}{F}\dot x^1\dot x^2+\frac{F_2}{2F}(\dot x^2)^2=0.$$

The first equation is the conserved‑momentum consequence of $g_{11}=1$ (translation invariance in $x^1$ when $F$ is independent of $x^1$), and the second is the standard equation for a conformal factor in the $x^2$‑direction. The structure is consistent with the metric being flat in the $x^1$‑direction and curved only through $F$ in the $x^2$‑direction, confirming the computation.

